Looking at this problem, I need to find the maximum number of beacons in a 106×106 grid such that for any two beacons, at least one of two conditions holds.

## Setting Up the Constraint

Let me denote two beacons at $(x,y)$ and $(x',y')$ with differences $a = x - x'$, $b = y - y'$. The conditions become:
- **Condition 1:** $a \geq -9$ and $b \geq -9$
- **Condition 2:** $a \leq 9$ and $b \leq 9$

A pair is **forbidden** (both conditions fail) when:
$$\bigl(a \leq -10 \text{ or } b \leq -10\bigr) \;\text{AND}\; \bigl(a \geq 10 \text{ or } b \geq 10\bigr)$$

Expanding (the impossible terms $a\leq{-10}\wedge a\geq{10}$ and $b\leq{-10}\wedge b\geq{10}$ drop out):

$$\text{Forbidden} \iff (a \leq -10 \text{ and } b \geq 10) \;\text{OR}\; (b \leq -10 \text{ and } a \geq 10)$$

In original coordinates: one beacon is **≥10 to the right and ≥10 below** the other (the "down-right" diagonal direction). The "up-right" direction is always allowed.

## Coordinate Transformation

Let $y^* = 107 - y$ (flip the $y$-axis). Then "$y$ decreases by ≥10" becomes "$y^*$ increases by ≥10." The forbidden condition becomes:

> No two points can have one **≥10 to the right and ≥10 above** the other (in $(x, y^*)$ coordinates).

This is a **dominance** relation with threshold 10.

## Upper Bound via Slope-1 Diagonals

Consider the slope-1 diagonals $x - y^* = k$ for $k \in \{-105, \ldots, 105\}$. Every grid point lies on exactly one such diagonal. The diagonal $k$ has length $106 - |k|$ (the number of valid points).

**Key claim:** On any single slope-1 diagonal, at most **10** points can be selected.

*Proof:* Two points $(x_1, y_1^*)$ and $(x_2, y_2^*)$ on diagonal $k$ satisfy $y_2^* - y_1^* = x_2 - x_1$. If $x_2 - x_1 \geq 10$, then $y_2^* - y_1^* \geq 10$ as well — both differences are ≥10 with the **same sign**, triggering the forbidden condition. So all selected points on one diagonal must have pairwise $x$-differences $\leq 9$, fitting in a window of 10 consecutive values. Hence at most 10 points. $\square$

Summing over all diagonals:
$$\text{Upper bound} = \sum_{k=-105}^{105} \min(106 - |k|,\; 10)$$

- For $|k| \leq 96$: length $\geq 10$, contribute 10 each. Count: $2(96)+1 = 193$ diagonals → $1930$.
- For $97 \leq |k| \leq 105$: length $= 106-|k|$ from 9 down to 1. Sum: $2(1+2+\cdots+9) = 90$.

$$\text{Upper bound} = 1930 + 90 = \mathbf{2020}$$

## Construction Achieving 2020

Use the **anti-diagonals** $x + y^* = c$ (slope $-1$). Two points on the same anti-diagonal have $y_2^* - y_1^* = -(x_2 - x_1)$, so differences always have **opposite signs** — never forbidden, regardless of spacing.

For two points on anti-diagonals $c_1 < c_2$: setting $d = y_2^* - y_1^*$, we get $x_2 - x_1 = (c_2 - c_1) - d$. The forbidden condition requires $d \geq 10$ and $(c_2-c_1) - d \geq 10$, i.e., $10 \leq d \leq c_2 - c_1 - 10$, which needs $c_2 - c_1 \geq 20$.

**Therefore, if all selected anti-diagonals lie within a range of 19 (i.e., span $\leq 19$), no pair is forbidden.**

Select all points on anti-diagonals $c \in \{97, 98, \ldots, 116\}$ (20 consecutive anti-diagonals, range $19 < 20$). This set is conflict-free.

**Counting the points:** The number of grid points with $97 \leq x + y^* \leq 116$ equals total points minus those outside this band:
- Points with $x + y^* \leq 96$: $\sum_{c=2}^{96}(c-1) = \frac{95 \cdot 96}{2} = 4560$
- Points with $x + y^* \geq 117$: $\sum_{c=117}^{212}(213-c) = \frac{96 \cdot 97}{2} = 4656$
- Selected: $106^2 - 4560 - 4656 = 11236 - 9216 = \mathbf{2020}$

## Conclusion

The upper bound and construction both yield **2020**, so the maximum number of beacons is:

$$\boxed{2020}$$

